---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.1 — while Loops &amp; The WhileLab Algorithms Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topics 2.1, 2.7 · 90-Minute Unified Build.</strong> A <code>while</code> loop
    repeats a block of code as long as a boolean condition remains <code>true</code> — making it
    the right tool whenever the number of iterations is <em>unknown in advance</em>. On the AP CS A
    exam, <code>while</code> loops appear in two classic forms: state-driven simulations (where a
    quantity like HP or energy ticks up and down until a threshold is crossed) and integer digit
    extraction (using <code>% 10</code> and <code>/ 10</code>).
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Spec-First Workflow (Download Both Files):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/WhileLab.java" download>WhileLab.java</a> — starter stub where you will
            assemble all four methods.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/WhileLabCheck.java" download>WhileLabCheck.java</a> — final acceptance
            verification suite (19 checks across 4 parts).
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; margin-bottom:0.25rem;">
        <strong>How to work today:</strong> On the AP exam, you get a specification and no check file.
        For each part below: (1) read the spec and trace the edge cases, (2) draft the individual code
        pieces in the assignment prompts, (3) combine your pieces into <code>WhileLab.java</code>,
        and (4) test your own edge cases by hand-tracing or in a scratch <code>main</code>
        <em>before</em> running <code>WhileLabCheck</code> at the end as final verification. Do not
        open <code>WhileLabCheck.java</code> to hunt for edge cases.
    </p>
    <pre><code>javac WhileLab.java WhileLabCheck.java
java WhileLabCheck</code></pre>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 1 · Spec, Edge Cases &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>roundsToSurvivePoison(int startingHP, int poisonPerRound, int regenPerRound)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <p>
        A hero enters a toxic swamp with <code>startingHP</code> hit points. During each round:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>First, poison strikes:</strong> subtract <code>poisonPerRound</code> from the
            hero's HP.</li>
        <li><strong>Second, check for survival:</strong> if the hero's HP is still strictly greater
            than <code>0</code>, their amulet restores <code>regenPerRound</code> HP. If their HP
            dropped to <code>0</code> or below from the poison tick, the hero dies immediately and
            regeneration does <strong>not</strong> occur.</li>
    </ol>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Pre-existing defeat:</strong> If <code>startingHP &lt;= 0</code>, the hero never
            endures a round — return <code>0</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Infinite survival:</strong> If the hero will survive <strong>forever</strong>,
            return <code>-1</code> without entering an infinite loop.</li>
        <li><strong>Otherwise:</strong> Return the exact integer number of rounds the hero is
            subjected to poison before dying.</li>
        <li><strong>Worked examples:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><code>(0, 5, 2)</code> &rarr; <code>0</code> (already dead before Round 1).</li>
                <li><code>(15, 4, 0)</code> &rarr; <code>4</code> (HP after each round: <code>11 &rarr; 7 &rarr; 3 &rarr; -1</code>).</li>
                <li><code>(10, 4, 2)</code> &rarr; <code>4</code> (R1: <code>10-4=6, +2=8</code>; R2: <code>8-4=4, +2=6</code>; R3: <code>6-4=2, +2=4</code>; R4: <code>4-4=0</code> dies before regen).</li>
                <li><code>(5, 5, 10)</code> &rarr; <code>1</code> (Round 1 poison drops HP to <code>0</code> before regen can fire).</li>
                <li><code>(10, 4, 4)</code> &rarr; <code>-1</code> (survives Round 1 with <code>6</code> HP, then regens back to <code>10</code> every round forever).</li>
                <li><code>(10, 0, 0)</code> &rarr; <code>-1</code> (non-positive poison never reduces HP).</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Edge-Case Analysis: Poison, HP, and Regen Guards</h3>
        <p>
            To avoid an infinite loop, most programmers immediately write this guard at the very top
            of <code>roundsToSurvivePoison</code>:
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Naive infinite-loop guard — why does this break on certain inputs?
if (regenPerRound &gt;= poisonPerRound) {
    return -1;
}</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>Trace <code>roundsToSurvivePoison(5, 5, 10)</code>: even though
                <code>regenPerRound &gt;= poisonPerRound</code>, what happens on Round 1 when poison
                hits <em>before</em> regeneration? Why does the naive guard above return
                <code>-1</code> when the spec requires <code>1</code>?</li>
            <li>What happens if <code>startingHP = 0</code> (or <code>-3</code>) and
                <code>regenPerRound = 10, poisonPerRound = 4</code>? Which condition must be checked
                first: <code>startingHP &lt;= 0</code> or infinite survival?</li>
            <li>State the exact conditions under which a hero with <code>startingHP &gt; 0</code>
                truly survives forever (consider both <code>poisonPerRound &lt;= 0</code> and what
                must survive Round 1's poison tick when <code>regenPerRound &gt;= poisonPerRound</code>)?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>roundsToSurvivePoison</code></h3>
        <p>
            Write each individual piece below before opening <code>WhileLab.java</code>.
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Entry Guards):</strong> Write the two <code>if</code> statements that
                handle (a) a hero who enters with <code>startingHP &lt;= 0</code> and (b) non-positive
                poison (<code>poisonPerRound &lt;= 0</code>) when the hero is alive?</li>
            <li><strong>Piece B (Round-1 vs. Infinite Regen Guard):</strong> Assuming
                <code>startingHP &gt; 0</code> and <code>poisonPerRound &gt; 0</code>, write the
                <code>if</code> logic that catches a Round-1 lethal tick
                (<code>startingHP - poisonPerRound &lt;= 0</code>) <em>before</em> returning
                <code>-1</code> when <code>regenPerRound &gt;= poisonPerRound</code>?</li>
            <li><strong>Piece C (Simulation Loop &amp; Return):</strong> Write the local variable
                initializations (<code>hp</code>, <code>rounds</code>), the <code>while</code> loop
                header, the exact intra-round order (increment <code>rounds</code>, subtract poison,
                conditional regen only if <code>hp &gt; 0</code>), and the final <code>return</code>?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Now combine Pieces A, B, and C in order inside <code>roundsToSurvivePoison</code> in
            <code>WhileLab.java</code>, then hand-trace <code>(10, 4, 2)</code> and
            <code>(5, 5, 10)</code> through your finished method.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Parts 2 &amp; 3 · AP Digit Extraction</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~35 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>sumRuneDigits(int runeCode)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>public static int sumRuneDigits(int runeCode)</code></li>
        <li><strong>Precondition:</strong> <code>runeCode &gt;= 0</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Behavior:</strong> Peel digits from right to left using <code>% 10</code>
            (rightmost digit) and <code>/ 10</code> (drop rightmost digit) and return the sum of all
            base-10 digits in <code>runeCode</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Worked examples:</strong> <code>4729 &rarr; 22</code> (<code>4 + 7 + 2 + 9</code>),
            <code>1005 &rarr; 6</code>, <code>8 &rarr; 8</code>, <code>0 &rarr; 0</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>sumRuneDigits</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Accumulator &amp; Loop Header):</strong> What accumulator variable
                do you initialize before the loop, what <code>while</code> condition peels all
                digits, and why does <code>sumRuneDigits(0)</code> naturally return <code>0</code>
                even if the loop runs <code>0</code> times?</li>
            <li><strong>Piece B (Loop Body &amp; Order):</strong> Write the loop body statements that
                extract the rightmost digit with <code>% 10</code>, add it to your accumulator, and
                shrink <code>runeCode</code> with <code>/ 10</code> — why would dividing by
                <code>10</code> <em>before</em> taking <code>% 10</code> break <code>4729</code>?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Combine Pieces A and B into <code>sumRuneDigits</code> in <code>WhileLab.java</code>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>countEvenDigits(int runeCode)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>public static int countEvenDigits(int runeCode)</code></li>
        <li><strong>Precondition:</strong> <code>runeCode &gt;= 0</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Behavior:</strong> Returns the count of even base-10 digits
            (<code>0, 2, 4, 6, 8</code>) in <code>runeCode</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Worked examples:</strong> <code>4826 &rarr; 4</code>,
            <code>13579 &rarr; 0</code>, <code>20405 &rarr; 4</code> (<code>0</code> is even!),
            <code>0 &rarr; 1</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Edge-Case Analysis: The Premature Return &amp; The Zero Edge Case</h3>
        <p>
            Look at two common bugs when students adapt <code>sumRuneDigits</code> into
            <code>countEvenDigits(int runeCode)</code>:
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Bug A: Premature else-return inside the loop
while (runeCode &gt; 0) {
    int digit = runeCode % 10;
    if (digit % 2 == 0) {
        count++;
    } else {
        return count;   // &lt;-- What does this do on 20405?!
    }
    runeCode /= 10;
}

// Bug B: What happens when runeCode starts at 0?
// How many times does while (runeCode &gt; 0) run when runeCode == 0?</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>In Bug A, why does putting <code>return count;</code> inside the loop stop after the
                very first odd digit (<code>5</code> in <code>20405</code>), never inspecting
                <code>0</code>, <code>4</code>, <code>0</code>, or <code>2</code>?</li>
            <li>The integer <code>0</code> has one digit, and <code>0</code> is an even number
                (<code>0 % 2 == 0</code>), so <code>countEvenDigits(0)</code> must return
                <code>1</code>. Why does a plain <code>while (runeCode &gt; 0)</code> loop return
                <code>0</code> for <code>countEvenDigits(0)</code>, and why didn't that same issue
                hurt <code>sumRuneDigits(0)</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>countEvenDigits</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Zero Edge-Case Guard):</strong> Write the exact <code>if</code>
                guard at the top of <code>countEvenDigits</code> for <code>runeCode == 0</code> —
                what does it return?</li>
            <li><strong>Piece B (Digit-Filter Loop):</strong> Write the counter initialization,
                <code>while</code> loop header, digit extraction, even check
                (<code>digit % 2 == 0</code>) with no premature return, shrinking step, and final
                <code>return</code>?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Combine Pieces A and B into <code>countEvenDigits</code> in <code>WhileLab.java</code>
            and trace <code>20405</code> and <code>0</code> by hand.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 4 · AP FRQ Synthesis Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>manaCrystalSteps(int startingEnergy)</code></h3>

    <h4>AP Specification</h4>
    <p>
        An unstable mana crystal oscillates according to the Collatz (Hailstone) rules until its
        energy stabilizes at <code>1</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>If <code>startingEnergy &lt;= 1</code>, it is already stable; return <code>0</code> steps.</li>
        <li>While energy is strictly greater than <code>1</code>:
            <ul>
                <li>If energy is <strong>even</strong>, divide it by <code>2</code>.</li>
                <li>If energy is <strong>odd</strong>, multiply it by <code>3</code> and add <code>1</code>.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>Count and return the total number of transformations required to reach <code>1</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Worked examples:</strong> <code>1 &rarr; 0</code>, <code>0 &rarr; 0</code>,
            <code>16 &rarr; 4</code> (<code>16 &rarr; 8 &rarr; 4 &rarr; 2 &rarr; 1</code>),
            <code>6 &rarr; 8</code> (<code>6 &rarr; 3 &rarr; 10 &rarr; 5 &rarr; 16 &rarr; 8 &rarr; 4 &rarr; 2 &rarr; 1</code>).</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Edge-Case Analysis: <code>if / else</code> vs. Two Separate <code>if</code>s Inside a Loop</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Suppose a student writes two separate <code>if</code> statements instead of
                <code>if / else</code> inside the <code>while</code> loop:
                <pre><code>if (energy % 2 != 0) {
    energy = energy * 3 + 1;
}
if (energy % 2 == 0) {
    energy = energy / 2;
}</code></pre>
                Trace what happens on a single iteration when <code>energy</code> starts at
                <code>3</code>. Why does the second <code>if</code> immediately fire in the same
                iteration, and how does <code>else</code> prevent that?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build Step-by-Step: Draft the Pieces for <code>manaCrystalSteps</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Piece A (Loop Condition &amp; Base Case):</strong> Write the
                <code>steps</code> initialization and <code>while</code> loop header — how does
                <code>while (startingEnergy &gt; 1)</code> automatically handle
                <code>startingEnergy &lt;= 1</code> without a separate <code>if</code> guard?</li>
            <li><strong>Piece B (One-Step Update &amp; Return):</strong> Write the
                <code>if / else</code> transformation, step-counter increment, and final
                <code>return</code> statement?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Combine Pieces A and B into <code>manaCrystalSteps</code> in <code>WhileLab.java</code>.
            Add a temporary <code>main</code> method (or trace by hand) to test your edge cases
            (<code>(0,5,2)</code>, <code>(5,5,10)</code>, <code>20405</code>, <code>0</code>,
            <code>6</code>), and <strong>only then</strong> run <code>java WhileLabCheck</code> as
            your final acceptance test.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Every method in <code>WhileLab.java</code> was built from your spec &amp; edge-case pieces and tested before running the check</li>
            <li><code>java WhileLabCheck</code> reports <strong><code>19 passed, 0 failed</code></strong></li>
            <li>You have completed the reflection and code-piece boxes above and downloaded your <code>.json</code> for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/while-loops.html">while Loops</a> — initialization, test, update, infinite loop guards</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/arithmetic.html">Arithmetic &amp; Modulo</a> — % 10 and / 10 digit extraction</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
